      "explanation": "Lưu Quang Vũ (1948–1988 - Phu Tho & Hanoi) and the golden age of modern Vietnamese spoken theater (Kịch nói):\n- **Lưu Quang Vũ**:\n  - The undisputed titan of modern Vietnamese playwriting; poet (married to legendary poet **Xuân Quỳnh**);\n  - Wrote over 50 explosive plays in the 1980s that single-handedly ignited the cultural awakening of the *Đổi Mới* era, with theaters in Hanoi and Saigon staging multiple productions simultaneously to packed audiences.\n- The Masterpiece: **«Hồn Trương Ba, da hàng thịt» (Truong Ba's Soul in the Butcher's Skin - 1984)**:\n  - Adapted from a folk tale; celestial bureaucrat Nam Tào makes a clerical error, causing gentle, elderly, chess-playing gardener Trương Ba to die prematurely;\n  - To correct the mistake, the gods place Trương Ba's noble soul into the recently deceased body of a coarse, gluttonous butcher (*anh hàng thịt*);\n  - **The Tragic Existential Climax**: Trương Ba's refined soul is corrupted by the butcher's physical appetites (lust, violence, greed), horrifying his wife and children;\n  - Trương Ba refuses to live in an alien body, declaring his immortal philosophical truth to deity Đế Thích: **«Không thể bên trong một đằng, bên ngoài một nẻo được. Tôi muốn được là tôi toàn vẹn!»** (One cannot be one thing on the inside and another on the outside. I want to be myself in my entirety!), choosing true death over a hypocritical, divided existence.",
